# halfcenter v57a -- VOID. DO NOT CITE ANY NUMBER FROM THE `hc > 0` ARMS.
# 2026-09-18. Gated on verify PASS (hash ad96f882becbee92, 22 experiments, 7.8 min).
#
# WHY IT IS VOID, not a refusal: every arm with halfcenter_gain > 0 has duty 0.00,
# anti-phase EXACTLY +0.000, peak 0.00 and SNR 0.00. Those are not measurements --
# they are what the statistics return on a constant input. The voice was silent.
# inhib20 and inhib60 are bit-identical to each other (rate 2.47, half-diff
# 4.97+/-0.56), as are both20/both60 -- two different gains giving identical output.
#
# TWO BUGS, BOTH MINE.
# 1. The term was ONE-SIDED. Both offsets were clamped to [0,1] and both SUBTRACTED,
#    so only the LOSING half was suppressed: net inhibitory, and positive feedback on
#    the difference (crushing the loser widens the gap, which crushes it further).
#    v32's comment warned a term scaling with what it derives from amplifies itself;
#    the clamp bounded the MAGNITUDE and left the SIGN STRUCTURE self-amplifying.
#    Fixed to zero-mean (subtract from one half, add to the other, sum exactly 0) --
#    which then SATURATED instead: duty 1.00, amplitude 0.999.
# 2. THE REAL ERROR, and it explains both failures. Splitting the module at its
#    midpoint (neuron 63 of 126, 9 groups of 14) puts the VOICING GATE (group 1) and
#    F1 (group 2) entirely in the lower half and AMPLITUDE (group 8) entirely in the
#    upper. So "reciprocal inhibition between the two halves" was competition between
#    two different CONTROL PARAMETERS, not two competing STATES of one variable.
#    Suppressing the loser killed voicing; boosting the winner pinned amplitude.
#    A half-center needs populations coding the SAME variable in OPPOSITE directions.
#    Fixed in v57b: the competition lives INSIDE one readout group (group 2 = F1),
#    where the two halves are a low-F1 and a high-F1 posture, and the zero-mean form
#    preserves the group's total activity so voicing and amplitude are untouched.
#    v57b measured: duty 0.48-0.50 and babble PASS across a 15x gain range
#    (0.20/0.60/1.20/3.00), with distinct hashes, so it is stable AND live.
#
# THE PRINTED VERDICT IS RETRACTED. It concluded "a rate code averaged over a
# population has no phase for two groups to be opposite in" -- drawn entirely from
# arms that produced no data. Nothing here supports it.
#
# WHAT DOES SURVIVE, from the two NON-void arms and it is worth having:
#   anti-phase corr = +0.838 +/- 0.003 (neither) and +0.824 +/- 0.004 (fatigue),
#   stable across both halves of the recording (+0.850/+0.825, +0.823/+0.792).
#   THE TWO HALVES OF THE LARYNX MOVE TOGETHER, very tightly. That is a direct
#   measurement of common-mode dominance and the baseline any half-center must beat.
#   NOTE the module-halves definition, so this number is {voicing,F1,..} vs
#   {..,amplitude}; the v57b readout measures within group 2 instead.
#
# INSTRUMENT FLAW: the "-208.3 SE" lines are an artefact of comparing 0.000 +/- 0.000
# against 0.824 +/- 0.004. A contrast needs a guard when either SE is ~0.
